# Telegram
# Both may be left blank (Phase 17): Telegram is then skipped entirely (no
# bot, no scheduled reports) while everything else still runs, so you can
# fill these in later from the web dashboard's Settings page — see
# WEB_ADDR/WEB_PASSWORD below — which restarts the service to apply them.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=your_bot_token_here
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=your_chat_id_here

# Data providers
# Optional but recommended: without it, quotes/news fall back to Yahoo, and
# fundamentals/financial-statement data (/fundamentals, and the fundamentals
# section in /check and /recommend) is unavailable entirely — Yahoo has no
# usable equivalent (its fundamentals endpoint requires a crumb/cookie
# handshake we don't implement). Leave this line blank/unset if you don't
# have a key — do NOT leave a placeholder string here, a non-empty value is
# treated as "configured" even if invalid.
# Can also be set from the web dashboard's Settings page once WEB_ADDR and
# WEB_PASSWORD are configured (Phase 17).
FINNHUB_API_KEY=your_finnhub_key_here

# Optional: Taiwan-market fundamentals (PER/PBR/dividend yield, quarterly
# EPS/revenue/gross margin, month-revenue YoY — see internal/data/finmind.go)
# via FinMind's free API, plus resolving a TW ticker's Chinese company name
# for display (e.g. "台積電(2330)" instead of a bare "2330") everywhere a TW
# ticker is shown. Leave blank/unset to disable — a TW ticker's
# /fundamentals and the fundamentals section in /check and /recommend simply
# have no data, and TW tickers display as bare numbers, same nil-degrade
# convention as FINNHUB_API_KEY above. Get a free token at
# https://finmindtrade.com/analysis/#/login (600 req/hr).
# Can also be set from the web dashboard's Settings page once WEB_ADDR and
# WEB_PASSWORD are configured (Phase 17).
FINMIND_TOKEN=your_finmind_token_here

# Optional: SEC EDGAR-derived US valuation percentile + cash-flow quality
# (Phase 23 PR6, see internal/data/sec.go), shown as briefing material in
# /check and /recommend — never a ranking/filter factor. No API key or
# registration, but SEC's edge filter TECHNICALLY REQUIRES the User-Agent to
# look like "identifier contact@email.com" (live-verified: a repo URL or a
# bare "Mozilla/5.0" both get a flat 403) — put a real, working email here,
# not a placeholder: SEC doesn't email you if something's wrong, it just
# silently blocks the IP. Leave blank/unset to disable entirely, same
# nil-degrade convention as FINNHUB_API_KEY above.
# Can also be set from the web dashboard's Settings page once WEB_ADDR and
# WEB_PASSWORD are configured (Phase 17).
SEC_USER_AGENT=your-app-name your-real-email@example.com

# Optional: Sinopac Securities (永豐證券) Shioaji API for broker-grade TW
# quotes/movers (ahead of Yahoo's unofficial, suffix-guessing chart API —
# see internal/data/shioaji.go) plus disposition/attention-stock filtering
# and auto-bookkeeping. Requires a running `shioaji server start` daemon
# (started separately by you — its token decryption needs your login, see
# deploy/shioaji.service) and points at that daemon's unix socket (default
# below) or a host:port. Leave blank/unset to disable — TW quotes/movers
# fall back to Yahoo/TWSE exactly as before, same nil-degrade convention as
# FINNHUB_API_KEY above.
# Can also be set from the web dashboard's Settings page once WEB_ADDR and
# WEB_PASSWORD are configured (Phase 17).
SHIOAJI_ADDR=/root/.shioaji/server-8080.sock

# Required by the `shioaji server` daemon itself, NOT read by Argus's Go code
# (grep internal/ finds no reference) — only relevant if you're running
# SHIOAJI_ADDR above. deploy/shioaji.service points the daemon's
# EnvironmentFile at this same .env file, so set these here and the daemon
# picks them up on `systemctl --user restart shioaji`. Get them from your
# Sinopac API key management page.
# Can also be set from the dashboard's Settings page (Phase 17), but saving
# there only restarts Argus — the daemon still needs its own
# `systemctl --user restart shioaji` before it picks up new keys.
SJ_API_KEY=your_sinopac_api_key_here
SJ_SEC_KEY=your_sinopac_secret_key_here

# Same daemon-only caveat as above. Default/unset runs the daemon in
# simulation mode (paper trading, no real orders); set to "true" once you've
# verified the API-test flow (see sinopac-shioaji-integration memory) and
# want /sinopac sync and /obuy-style flows to hit your real account.
SJ_PRODUCTION=

# Optional, only used when SHIOAJI_ADDR is set: comma-separated tickers the
# /sinopac sync auto-bookkeeping job should never propose trades for — your
# 定期定額 (periodic investment) tickers, e.g. "0050,006208". Their scheduled
# buys aren't discrete manual decisions worth a pending-action confirmation.
# Can also be set from the web dashboard's Settings page once WEB_ADDR and
# WEB_PASSWORD are configured (Phase 17).
SINOPAC_SKIP_TICKERS=

# Optional, only used when SHIOAJI_ADDR is set: set to "true" to let the
# scheduled sync (14:00 CST Mon-Fri, see scheduler.AddSinopacSync) create
# real pending actions. Unset/anything else keeps it dry-run (list-only, no
# pending_actions rows) — the default until PositionDetail/ProfitLoss's
# price-field semantics (per-share vs. total trade amount) have been
# reconciled against your actual brokerage statement. The manually-typed
# `/sinopac sync` command always runs live regardless of this flag, since
# typing it is already the confirmation this gate exists to wait for.
# Can also be set from the web dashboard's Settings page once WEB_ADDR and
# WEB_PASSWORD are configured (Phase 17).
SINOPAC_SYNC_LIVE=

# LLM
# Uses your Claude Pro/Max subscription via the local `claude` CLI login
# instead of a billed API key. Run `claude` once on this machine and sign in
# before starting the bot; no ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is needed (and must NOT be
# set here or in your shell env, or the ACP agent will bill your API account
# instead of using the subscription). Requires Node.js (npx) to be installed.
# Model aliases: "opus", "sonnet", "haiku", or a full model ID. Leave unset
# to use the agent's own default.
CLAUDE_RECOMMEND_MODEL=opus
CLAUDE_CHECK_MODEL=sonnet
CLAUDE_CHAT_MODEL=sonnet
# Optional: point at a globally-installed `claude-agent-acp` binary instead
# of paying npx's resolution overhead on every call:
#   npm install -g @agentclientprotocol/claude-agent-acp
# CLAUDE_ACP_COMMAND=claude-agent-acp

# Optional Antigravity fallback (used only if every Claude call fails, e.g.
# usage cap hit). Uses your Google Antigravity subscription via OAuth login
# to the `agy` CLI, no API key. Off by default and NOT a drop-in-safe
# feature: `agy`'s non-interactive mode has no read-only equivalent and will
# auto-approve any tool call it decides to make, so this always runs with
# --sandbox to contain that to an isolated container rather than the host —
# read AntigravityProvider's doc comment in internal/llm before enabling.
# Requires the VPS to have a working sandbox/container runtime for `agy`.
# ANTIGRAVITY_ENABLED=true
# ANTIGRAVITY_MODEL=

# Exit discipline (Phase 3.8, daily-report only — no intraday/at-price
# monitoring). Positive percentages; set either to 0 to disable that check.
# STOP_LOSS_PCT alerts once when a position's unrealized loss crosses this
# threshold (default below the cost basis). TRAILING_STOP_PCT alerts once
# when the price falls this far from the highest close since the position
# was first opened. Both dedup via signal_states: they alert once per
# breach and reset once the position recovers back above the threshold.
#
# TRAILING_STOP_PCT was 15 until it was measured (2026-08-26). Replaying the
# random-entry control through the exit engine at 8/12/15/18/25 gives a
# monotonically better result the looser it gets, in all four samples
# (US/TW x in/out-of-sample). A PAIRED bootstrap on the identical entries —
# only the ~5-20% of trades whose exit actually moves carry information —
# puts 15 -> 18 at +0.05 to +0.20% per trade, 2.3-4.0 sigma in every sample.
# Reproduce with cmd/strategyscan: -dump-trades at two -trailing-pct values.
#
# 18 -> 25 measures better still in three of the four samples, and is NOT
# taken: what the backtest maximizes is mean return per trade, which is not
# what this setting is for. By 25 only ~1% of exits come from the trailing
# stop at all, and for real (non-paper) positions this threshold is an ALERT,
# not an exit — a 25% giveback is too late to be a warning. 18 is the loosest
# value that still earns its place; going further is a risk-appetite call,
# not one the return numbers can make.
STOP_LOSS_PCT=10
TRAILING_STOP_PCT=18

# TW's own versions of the two above (Phase 13 §7) — TW trades under a daily
# ±10% price limit, so the US-calibrated 10 would trip on an ordinary limit
# move. 0 (or unset) falls back to STOP_LOSS_PCT/TRAILING_STOP_PCT.
# TRAILING_STOP_PCT_TW has always been 18 and the measurement above agrees
# with it; the US value moved to match, not the other way round.
#
# STOP_LOSS_PCT/_TW are deliberately NOT calibrated by that study: changing
# the stop distance changes paper.SuggestShares' position size, so two stop
# widths produce different notional exposure and their % returns are not
# comparable without R-normalizing first. That is a separate measurement.
STOP_LOSS_PCT_TW=12
TRAILING_STOP_PCT_TW=18

# Optional ATR(14)-based trailing-stop distance (Phase 3.8 追加項, see
# docs/phase-3.8-atr-trailing-stop.md). Default 0 disables it — behavior is
# then identical to before this setting existed. When > 0, the effective
# threshold becomes min(TRAILING_STOP_PCT, TRAILING_STOP_ATR_MULT x ATR(14) /
# peak x 100): TRAILING_STOP_PCT still caps worst-case drawdown tolerance,
# while the ATR-based distance tightens it for lower-volatility tickers.
# 3 is the textbook chandelier-exit multiplier. Falls back to
# TRAILING_STOP_PCT alone when ATR can't be computed for a ticker.
# TRAILING_STOP_ATR_MULT=3

# Optional ATR(14)-based take-profit for the live paper account only (2026-08-08
# strategy tuning, docs/phase-11-paper-strategy-tuning.md §3) — real-money
# positions still only exit via SELL/STOP_LOSS_PCT/TRAILING_STOP_PCT above.
# Default 0 disables it (paper account only ever exits via llm_sell/stop/
# trailing, same as before this setting existed). When > 0, a paper BUY sets
# a target = entry + PAPER_TAKE_PROFIT_ATR_MULT x ATR(14), and MarkClose exits
# at that target before checking the trailing stop — an R-multiple relative to
# the 2xATR14 initial stop (stopCandidateATRMult), so e.g. 2 means "take
# profit at 1R". No fixed-%% fallback: without ATR there's no R to be a
# multiple of, so that position simply has no take-profit target.
#
# Measured 2026-08-26 and left disabled deliberately. Sweeping 2/3/4/6 ATR
# against the random-entry control costs return at every value, in all four
# samples, and the tighter the worse: on US out-of-sample, 2 ATR lifts the
# win rate from 41.6% to 59.6% while cutting mean return from +2.14% to
# +0.98%. Taking profit early buys the appearance of winning often and sells
# the returns — this book's returns are entirely right-tail driven. Don't
# uncomment this without re-running the sweep first.
# PAPER_TAKE_PROFIT_ATR_MULT=2

# Optional R-based position-sizing suggestion on BUY recommendations (Phase
# 3.11 PR1, see docs/phase-3.11-trade-risk-management.md). Default 0/unset
# disables the feature entirely — no sizing line is shown. When set (e.g.
# 1.0 = risk at most 1% of account value per trade), each BUY recommendation
# with a computable ATR(14) gets a bot-computed suggested share count
# (account value = latest net worth snapshot + declared /cash balance, stop
# reference = price - 2xATR14). Never sent to the LLM to compute — this is
# deterministic Go arithmetic only.
# RISK_PCT_PER_TRADE=1.0

# Storage
DB_PATH=data/argus.db
# Log file, rotated daily at midnight (Taiwan time) and kept for ~7 days.
LOG_PATH=data/argus.log
# Log level: debug, info (default), warn, or error.
LOG_LEVEL=info
# Daily SQLite backup (VACUUM INTO, 06:00 Taiwan time) and how many days of
# backups to keep before older ones are pruned.
BACKUP_DIR=data/backups
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14

# Language
# "zh" (Traditional Chinese, default) or "en". Controls both the bot's own
# UI copy (internal/i18n) and the language Claude is asked to answer in for
# /check, /recommend, and chat. Anything unrecognized (including unset)
# falls back to "zh".
BOT_LANGUAGE=zh

# Web dashboard (Phase 5, see docs/phase-5-web-dashboard.md)
# Leave blank/unset to disable (default) — do not expose this publicly, it
# has no HTTPS of its own by design; access it via Tailscale or an SSH
# tunnel to the VPS instead. Example:
#   WEB_ADDR=127.0.0.1:8090
WEB_ADDR=

# Web trade-input write auth (Phase 10, see docs/phase-10-web-trade-input.md)
# Blank/unset disables writes entirely — the dashboard stays read-only, same
# as before this phase. Set a password to enable the buy/sell/stop/
# watchlist forms; this is a Tailscale-internal deterrent, not a public-facing
# login, so pick anything reasonable rather than treating it as a real secret.
WEB_PASSWORD=

# LLM input transparency (Phase 19, see docs/phase-19-llm-transparency.md)
# Set to "true" to expose the /llm audit-trail page (every /recommend and
# daily-report call's full input + raw model reply). Off by default — the
# data is recorded either way, this only gates reading it back over HTTP.
WEB_LLM_AUDIT=
